### What is order-agnostic binary search?

Order-agnostic binary search works on a sorted array whose direction, ascending or descending, is not known in advance. The algorithm first detects the direction by comparing the first and last elements, then runs standard binary search with the comparison logic adjusted to match the detected direction.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class OrderAgnosticBinarySearch {

    public static int search(int[] arr, int key) {
        int start = 0;
        int end = arr.length - 1;
        boolean ascending = arr[start] < arr[end];

        while (start <= end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;

            if (arr[mid] == key) {
                return mid;
            }

            if (ascending) {
                if (key < arr[mid]) end = mid - 1;
                else start = mid + 1;
            } else {
                if (key > arr[mid]) end = mid - 1;
                else start = mid + 1;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] ascending = {1000, 2000, 3000, 4000, 5000};
        int[] descending = {5000, 4000, 3000, 2000, 1000};

        System.out.println("Ascending, find 3000: " + search(ascending, 3000));
        System.out.println("Descending, find 3000: " + search(descending, 3000));
    }
}
```

```text
Ascending, find 3000: 2
Descending, find 3000: 2
```

### How does direction detection work?

Direction is detected by a single comparison of the endpoints: `arr[start] < arr[end]` means ascending, otherwise descending. This check costs O(1) and is done once before the loop.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class DirectionDetection {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] asc = {1000, 2000, 3000, 4000, 5000};
        int[] desc = {5000, 4000, 3000, 2000, 1000};
        int[] equal = {3000, 3000, 3000};

        System.out.println("asc[0] < asc[4]: " + (asc[0] < asc[4]));      // true
        System.out.println("desc[0] < desc[4]: " + (desc[0] < desc[4]));   // false
        System.out.println("equal[0] < equal[2]: " + (equal[0] < equal[2])); // false (irrelevant)
    }
}
```

```text
asc[0] < asc[4]: true
desc[0] < desc[4]: false
equal[0] < equal[2]: false
```

### How does the comparison flip work?

In an ascending array, when `key < arr[mid]`, the key is to the left, so `end = mid - 1`. In a descending array, when `key < arr[mid]`, the key is to the right (because smaller values are on the right in a descending array), so `start = mid + 1`. The comparison operators are swapped based on direction.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ComparisonFlip {

    public static int search(int[] arr, int key) {
        int start = 0, end = arr.length - 1;
        boolean asc = arr[start] < arr[end];
        while (start <= end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == key) return mid;
            if (asc) {
                System.out.println("  ASC: mid=" + mid + " arr[mid]=" + arr[mid]
                        + (key < arr[mid] ? " key left" : " key right"));
                if (key < arr[mid]) end = mid - 1; else start = mid + 1;
            } else {
                System.out.println("  DESC: mid=" + mid + " arr[mid]=" + arr[mid]
                        + (key > arr[mid] ? " key left" : " key right"));
                if (key > arr[mid]) end = mid - 1; else start = mid + 1;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Searching descending [5000,4000,3000,2000,1000] for 4000:");
        System.out.println("Found at: " + search(new int[]{5000, 4000, 3000, 2000, 1000}, 4000));
    }
}
```

```text
Searching descending [5000,4000,3000,2000,1000] for 4000:
  DESC: mid=2 arr[mid]=3000 key left
Found at: 1
```

### What if all values are equal?

If every element is the same, direction detection returns false (not ascending), but it does not matter because the key either equals every element (found immediately) or does not exist. Either branch handles it correctly.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class AllEqualCase {

    public static int search(int[] arr, int key) {
        int start = 0, end = arr.length - 1;
        boolean asc = arr[start] < arr[end];
        while (start <= end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == key) return mid;
            if (asc) { if (key < arr[mid]) end = mid - 1; else start = mid + 1; }
            else { if (key > arr[mid]) end = mid - 1; else start = mid + 1; }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] allSame = {3000, 3000, 3000, 3000};
        System.out.println("Find 3000: " + search(allSame, 3000));
        System.out.println("Find 5000: " + search(allSame, 5000));
    }
}
```

```text
Find 3000: 1
Find 5000: -1
```

### What about the overflow-safe midpoint?

Always compute `mid = start + (end - start) / 2` instead of `(start + end) / 2` to avoid integer overflow when both indices are large. This applies to every binary search variant.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MidSafety {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int start = 1_500_000_000, end = 2_000_000_000;
        int bad = (start + end) / 2;
        int good = start + (end - start) / 2;
        System.out.println("Unsafe: " + bad);
        System.out.println("Safe:   " + good);
    }
}
```

```text
Unsafe: -397483648
Safe:   1750000000
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single-element array, a two-element ascending array, a two-element descending array, and searching for a value not present.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class OrderAgnosticEdgeCases {

    public static int search(int[] arr, int key) {
        int start = 0, end = arr.length - 1;
        boolean asc = arr[start] < arr[end];
        while (start <= end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == key) return mid;
            if (asc) { if (key < arr[mid]) end = mid - 1; else start = mid + 1; }
            else { if (key > arr[mid]) end = mid - 1; else start = mid + 1; }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Single: " + search(new int[]{5000}, 5000));
        System.out.println("Two asc: " + search(new int[]{1000, 2000}, 2000));
        System.out.println("Two desc: " + search(new int[]{2000, 1000}, 1000));
        System.out.println("Not found: " + search(new int[]{1000, 3000, 5000}, 4000));
    }
}
```

```text
Single: 0
Two asc: 1
Two desc: 1
Not found: -1
```

### How does this relate to other Modified Binary Search problems?

Order-agnostic binary search is the first and simplest Modified Binary Search problem. It introduces the pattern of adapting standard binary search to a slightly different situation. The same idea of adjusting comparisons appears in ceiling/floor finding, number range, and bitonic array search.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should note that the direction detection from endpoints assumes at least two distinct values. If only one value exists, direction is irrelevant and the standard match check handles it. The single-check detection keeps the algorithm at O(log n) total with O(1) space. This problem sets the foundation for every Modified Binary Search variant that follows.
